WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:06.030
The choice of features can have a huge impact on your learning algorithm's performance.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:11.030
In fact, for many practical applications, choosing or entering the right features is

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:14.510
a critical step to making the algorithm work well.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:18.670
In this video, let's take a look at how you can choose or engineer the most appropriate

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.670
features for your learning algorithm.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.870
Let's take a look at feature engineering by revisiting the example of predicting the

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.630
price of a house.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.510
Say you have two features for each house.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:37.670
X1 is the width of the lot size of the plot of land that the house is built on.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:42.670
This in real estate is also called the frontage of the lot.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:48.950
And the second feature, X2 is the depth of the lot size of, let's assume, the rectangular

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.990
plot of land that the house was built on.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:57.310
Given these two features, X1 and X2, you might build a model like this, where f of

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:08.190
x is w1 x1 plus w2 x2 plus b, where x1 is the frontage or width and x2 is the depth.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.510
And this model might work okay.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:14.550
But here's another option for how you might choose a different way to use these features

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.310
in the model that could be even more effective.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.550
You might notice that the area of the land can be calculated as the frontage or width

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.630
times the depth.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:30.350
And you may have an intuition that the area of the land is more predictive of the price

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:34.190
than the frontage and depth as separate features.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:39.930
So you might define a new feature, X3 as X1 times X2.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:45.510
So this new feature X3 is equal to the area of the plot of land.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:55.230
From this feature, you can then have a model f wb of x equals w1 x1 plus w2 x2 plus w3 x3

00:01:55.230 --> 00:02:02.790
plus b, so that the model can now choose parameters w1, w2, and w3 depending on whether the data

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:09.510
shows the frontage or the depth, or the area X3 of the lot turns out to be the most important

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:12.990
thing for predicting the price of the house.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:19.510
What we just did, creating a new feature is an example of what's called feature engineering,

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:24.750
in which you might use your knowledge or intuition about the problem to design new features,

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:29.630
usually by transforming or combining the original features of the problem in order to make

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:33.830
it easier for the learning algorithm to make accurate predictions.

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:38.590
So depending on what insights you may have into the application, rather than just taking

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:43.990
the features that you happen to start off with, sometimes by defining new features,

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.750
you might be able to get a much better model.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:51.150
So that's feature engineering.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:55.590
And it turns out that there's one flavor of feature engineering that allow you to fit

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:00.510
not just straight lines, but curves, nonlinear functions to your data.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.150
Let's take a look in the next video at how you can do that.
